From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Frida Hou <Frida-a@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 01:11:26 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] [GDN] spec-decode verify records + commit kernels (no
 per-draft state writes)

Triton kernels for GDN speculative decoding that keep one recurrent state per
request instead of one per draft position:

- gdn_recover_verify: runs the same per-token recurrence as
  fused_sigmoid_gating_delta_rule_update (same code and tiling, state in
  registers) but, instead of storing the fp32 state after every token, writes
  per-token records (u = beta (v - S k) fp32 [HV, V], raw k act-dtype [H, K],
  g fp32 [HV]) into a ring in the request's Mamba slot.
- GDNRecoverCommitGroup.commit: after sampling, folds the accepted records
  into the checkpoint state in forward order (fma(u, k, S * exp(g)), the
  contraction the verify uses) for all layers of a cache group in one launch.
  Hooks: per-request dst slot and block-boundary state (align mode), row
  indices into the batch's num_sampled, window W > 1 with ring trackers
  (records stay pending across steps), forced folds.
- State codec dispatch points gdn_spec_load_state / gdn_spec_store_state on a
  constexpr FMT (fp32 implemented; bf16/int16_row/int8_row/int8_had32
  reserved) with per-head padded rows VP.

Tests compare outputs and committed states bit-exactly against the Triton
per-token reference (K+1 = 4, 8; batch 1/4/32; ragged; W = 1/2/3) and report
the difference to the CUDA fused MTP kernel. Benchmark script included.

